// toolbox/osutils.cla: curated OS Utilities / Gestalt declarations
// (Inside Macintosh II "OS Utilities"; Gestalt from IM VI ch3).
//
// Provenance: Gestalt (0xA1AD). AIncludes/Gestalt.a:43 contributes the
// TRAP WORD only (`_Gestalt: OPWORD $A1AD` -- no register block at all,
// so it is silent on the calling convention). The register evidence comes
// from decoding CIncludes/Gestalt.h:64-69's inline words, the same way
// Delay is decoded below, confirmed by an on-hardware probe (see below) --
// which is the ONLY way to get it right, because the `#pragma parameter`
// line alone is actively misleading here.
//
//   Gestalt.h:  #pragma parameter __D0 Gestalt(__D0, __A1)
//               Gestalt(OSType selector, long *response)
//                   TWOWORDINLINE(0xA1AD, 0x2288);
//
// The pragma describes the INLINE GLUE's parameter passing, not the raw
// trap's: `response` (a `long *`) goes in A1 because the glue's SECOND
// word needs it there. Decode that second word -- 0x2288 is the raw 68k
// instruction `MOVE.L A0,(A1)` -- and the trap's own contract falls out:
// the ROM leaves the RESPONSE VALUE in **A0** and the OSErr in D0, and
// Apple's glue is what copies A0 through the caller's `response`
// pointer afterwards. The trap itself never writes through A1.
//
// So Retro68's multiversal/defs/Gestalt.yaml (`responsep` `register:
// Out<A0>`) was RIGHT all along, and this catalog's earlier
// `reg(d0: selector, a1: response) ret d0` declaration was wrong: it
// bound A1 as an INPUT (harmless, the trap ignores it), read D0 as the
// result (correct -- that IS the OSErr), and simply DROPPED the response
// value sitting in A0. `*response` was never written at all, so every
// caller read back whatever uninitialized heap garbage its freshly
// NewPtr'd response slot happened to contain -- a clean noErr paired
// with a junk value, passing or failing purely on heap luck. Proven on
// real hardware (pack3-standardfile Task 5a, Mini vMac/System 6.0.7):
// poking a 0x11223344 sentinel into the response slot before the call
// and reading it back afterwards returns 0x11223344 unchanged, while
// `ret a0` on the same trap returns 0x0607, a real system version. (0x0607
// rather than the 0x0608 this repo's `System 6.0.8.dsk` name suggests:
// 6.0.8 was a printing-only update over 6.0.7 and left gestaltSystemVersion
// at 0x0607. Not a mismatch, and not something to "fix".)
//
// A Clarus `reg` extern names exactly ONE result register, and has no
// way to emit Apple's second glue word, so the trap is transcribed
// TWICE -- GestaltErr reads D0 (the OSErr), GestaltValue reads A0 (the
// response). Gestalt is a pure query with no side effects, so calling it
// once for each register is safe; check GestaltErr first, then read
// GestaltValue. Same "faithful, if reduced, transcription" the Delay
// note below spells out at length, for the same TWOWORDINLINE reason.
//
// Multiversal stays UNTRUSTED WHEN CITED ALONE file-wide in this catalog
// (see below) -- every multiversal-only citation here has been
// re-checked directly against Apple's own Universal Interfaces 3.4
// (Retro68/InterfacesAndLibraries/Interfaces), the authoritative source,
// and is superseded by that re-check. Gestalt is the standing reminder
// that the failure mode runs BOTH ways: a generated def can be wrong,
// and so can a hand reading of a `#pragma parameter` line that describes
// glue rather than the trap. Decode the inline words.
//
// SysBeep proven verbatim against runtime/clarus/ui.cla's UiSysBeep
// (0xA9C8); re-confirmed directly against CIncludes/Sound.h:1487
// `SysBeep(short duration) ONEWORDINLINE(0xA9C8)` (SysBeep moved out of
// OSUtils.h into Sound.h in this SDK revision, per that header's own
// comment) and AIncludes/Sound.a:1156 OPWORD $A9C8 -- no register
// pragma, bit 11 set (0xA9C8 & 0x0800 != 0): plain pascal, no `reg`,
// matches.
//
// Delay (0xA03B) was originally checked only against Retro68
// multiversal/defs/OSUtil.yaml (`n` register A0 in, `ftp` register
// `Out<D0>`). Directly re-confirmed against CIncludes/OSUtils.h:352-358
// and AIncludes/OSUtils.a:320, with one correction to the story
// multiversal told: Apple's real signature is TWO parameters, `Delay
// (unsigned long numTicks, unsigned long *finalTicks)`, pragma
// `Delay(__A0, __A1)` (note: no leading `__D0` -- the Pascal-level call
// is a void PROCEDURE, not a function), glued as `TWOWORDINLINE(0xA03B,
// 0x2280)`. Decoding that second word: `0x2280` is the raw 68k
// instruction `MOVE.L D0,(A1)` -- ordinary compiler-emitted glue that
// runs AFTER the trap returns, storing D0 through the caller-supplied
// `finalTicks` pointer in A1. That proves the raw ROM trap itself, before
// this glue instruction executes, already leaves the final tick count in
// D0 and never reads A1 -- A1/finalTicks exists only to satisfy the
// Pascal VAR-parameter ABI at the call site, not the trap's own
// contract. This catalog's declaration below (one parameter, `ret d0`,
// no A1 binding at all) is therefore a faithful, if reduced,
// transcription: it reads the exact same D0 value Apple's glue would
// also copy into `*finalTicks`, just without bothering to also write it
// through a second pointer this codebase never needed. numTicks-in-A0
// matches on both accounts.

const gestaltSystemVersion: int = 0x73797376

external func GestaltErr(selector: int): word = trap 0xA1AD reg(d0: selector) ret d0
external func GestaltValue(selector: int): int = trap 0xA1AD reg(d0: selector) ret a0
external func Delay(numTicks: int): int = trap 0xA03B reg(a0: numTicks) ret d0
external func SysBeep(duration: word) = trap 0xA9C8

// ---- Date-Time Utilities (IM II "Date and Time Operations") ----
//
// Provenance: declarations verified against Interfaces/CIncludes/
// DateTimeUtils.h and Interfaces/AIncludes/DateTimeUtils.a (2026-08-10
// datetime-instrumentation phase). GetDateTime is deliberately ABSENT:
// it is not a trap at all -- Apple's glue is TWOWORDINLINE(0x20B8,
// 0x020C), a MOVE.L of the low-memory Time global ($020C) through the
// caller's pointer -- and Clarus programs should call the builtin now()
// instead, which performs exactly that read on the native lane.
//
// _SecondsToDate/_DateToSeconds are the documented EXCEPTION to the
// reference's bit-11 convention rule: Toolbox-range trap words (bit 11
// set) with REGISTER-based contracts. AIncludes/DateTimeUtils.a is
// explicit ("secs => D0, d => A0" over OPWORD $A9C6), and the CIncludes
// pragma (#pragma parameter SecondsToDate(__D0, __A0), ONEWORDINLINE)
// shows no glue words at all -- the raw trap takes registers.

extern record DateTimeRec {
    year: word
    month: word
    day: word
    hour: word
    minute: word
    second: word
    dayOfWeek: word
}

// _ReadDateTime (0xA039): re-reads the battery-backed clock chip
// (slow, serial) and rewrites the Time global; boot-time resync, not a
// routine time read. A0 = VAR secs (unsigned long), OSErr <= D0.
external func ReadDateTime(time: ptr): int = trap 0xA039 reg(a0: time) ret d0

// _SecondsToDate / Secs2Date (0xA9C6): unsigned seconds => D0,
// DateTimeRec pointer => A0, no result. Fills year (full year), month
// 1-12, day 1-31, hour 0-23, minute, second, dayOfWeek (1 = Sunday).
external func SecondsToDate(secs: int, d: ptr) = trap 0xA9C6 reg(d0: secs, a0: d)

// _DateToSeconds / Date2Secs (0xA9C7): DateTimeRec pointer => A0,
// unsigned seconds <= D0. Tolerates out-of-range fields (month 13 rolls
// into the next year) -- the ROM's own date arithmetic hook.
external func DateToSeconds(d: ptr): int = trap 0xA9C7 reg(a0: d) ret d0

// (SetDateTime is deliberately absent -- writing the clock is nothing a
// Clarus program needs; if it ever is, 0xA03A takes D0 = seconds, OSErr
// <= D0.)
